Z||000000||86SE||10-25-2000||09:53 PM||||I havent been on the forum lately (not that anyone noticed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) but now I finally have enough money to start my winter Fiero project, a 3.4 liter pushrod camaro engine conversion.  I just hope it dosent cost more than $1200.<P>I should be ordering my engine this week, if not tomorrow.  I can't wait to feel the power of a newer, lower mileage, bigger engine, in the SAME car.<P>I'll keep you all posted on my progress. Wish me luck, because im sure i'll need it, along with a new set of knuckles....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||10
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||10-26-2000||10:06 AM||||Good luck man. If I had to do it over again, I would have gone w/ the 3.4. My 3.2 wound up arround $1800 because I couldn't get a core for the crank and all 6 connecting rods were fried. $1200 will be pretty tight budget for the 3.4 (I thought the crate alone was $1600?) but if you can do the machine work yourself for the starter, you'll be all good! With as much fun as the 3.2 is, I'm sure you'll love the 3.4. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||10-26-2000||12:25 PM||||The engine I am buying is used from TA Creations for $400+$150 shipping.  It will have around 50K miles, but is complete with injectors.  All I have to buy is a flexplate ($40 new from Advanced Auto) and i'll be set.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||4
